## How Much Should You Tip for a Pedicure?

Fifteen percent is the floor. Twenty percent is safer, and it is what most nail technicians expect for a standard pedicure with no complications. The <strong>15-20% rule</strong> is the baseline across nail salons in the United States, and it applies whether the service was a basic polish change or a full spa pedicure with callus treatment.

The confusion is not the range itself. It is that every article answering <strong>how much to tip for a pedicure</strong> seems to quote a slightly different one, 10-20% here, 15-25% there, a flat 20% somewhere else, and none of them resolve to a single answer you can act on. Here is the resolution: 20% is the number to default to unless there is a specific reason to move off it. Fifteen percent is not rude. It is the acceptable low end for a service that was fine but unremarkable. It becomes a problem only when it drifts lower than that, or when it is applied out of guilt rather than genuine intent to shortchange the visit.

Etiquette experts frame this as a floor rather than a ceiling. One well-known etiquette authority has described 20% as the baseline expectation for good service, moving toward 25% or more once the service crosses into excellent. That framing matters. It treats 20% as the starting point for solid work, not a stretch goal. Nail technicians rely on tips for a meaningful share of their income, and the gap between 15% and 20% on a $50 pedicure is five dollars, not a difference worth agonising over at the counter.

