## Do You Tip on the Discounted or Original Price?

Tip on the original price, not the discounted one. This is one of the most common exceptions people search for when working out <strong>how much to tip for a pedicure</strong> paid with a coupon. If a $60 pedicure drops to $45 with a coupon or gift card, the tip is still calculated from $60, which means $9 to $12 rather than $6.75 to $9.

This is the rule people get wrong most often, worth naming plainly because the logic is easy to miss. The technician still performed a full $60 service. The coupon reduced what the salon was paid, not what the technician earned in labour and skill. Tipping on the reduced number effectively cuts a technician's expected income for a job that took identical time and care regardless of the discount applied. Etiquette guidance is unambiguous on this: tip on the full pre-discount total whenever a coupon, promotion, or gift certificate is involved.

A phrase circulates around exactly this situation, don't be that person who tips on the reduced price, and it exists because everyone in the service industry has seen it play out. A gift card covering the full cost of a pedicure is the clearest version of this trap. The service still cost something in time and skill even though the card balance reads zero, and the tip should reflect the value of that service, not the amount that left a wallet.

One exception is worth flagging. If a third-party discount platform reduces what the salon itself is paid, and the technician's take is affected beyond a standard in-house coupon, tipping slightly above the usual range on the discounted price is a fair adjustment. That situation is rare. For the ordinary in-salon coupon or loyalty discount, original price is the rule, no exceptions worth making.

