## Should You Tip More for a Gel or Spa Pedicure?

A little, yes. Gel pedicures and deluxe spa tiers involve more time, more product, and more technical skill than a basic polish change, and the higher end of the 15-20% range, or slightly above it, reflects that extra labour.

The price difference already does some of this work automatically. A gel pedicure costs more than a basic one, so a flat 20% naturally produces a bigger dollar tip without any extra thinking. Where the adjustment matters is at the margin, when soak-off removal from a previous gel set takes real extra time, or when a spa pedicure includes a hot stone or paraffin add-on that runs well past a standard appointment. In those cases, nudging toward 25% acknowledges effort the base price tier doesn't fully capture.

Nail art sits in a similar category. Intricate design work, hand-painted detail, or embellishments add meaningful time to a service, and tipping toward the higher end, sometimes above 20%, is standard practice when the design portion of a visit ran long. Is it normal to tip 25% for a pedicure with nail art? Yes, particularly when the art itself was the reason the appointment took longer than a routine visit.

