## Cash vs Card: Does It Change How Much You Should Tip?

The percentage stays the same either way. What changes is how much of that tip actually reaches the technician, which is why cash is still the preferred method across the industry.

Cash is king in nail salons for a practical reason: it reaches the technician immediately and in full. A card tip has to move through payroll, and depending on how a salon processes transactions, a portion can be absorbed by card processing fees before it reaches the person who did the service. Merchant fees on card transactions commonly run from around 1.5% to 2.5% or higher, and some salons factor that into how card tips get distributed. Cash sidesteps all of it.

There is a tax and paperwork angle too, one salon owners point to directly. Cash tips involve less administrative overhead for the business, part of why so many salons post signage encouraging it or accept cash-only gratuities even when the service itself is paid by card.

<strong>Tip pooling</strong> is a separate consideration worth understanding here. Tip pooling is the practice of combining tips from multiple staff members into a shared pool that gets divided among the team, rather than each technician keeping the full amount earned individually. Tipping by card at a salon that pools tips means the gratuity may not go entirely to the person who worked on your feet. Asking upfront whether tips are pooled or kept individually is a fair question, and most front desks answer it plainly.

None of this makes card tips wrong or insufficient. If cash isn't an option, tip by card at the intended percentage without hesitation. It simply means cash is the more direct route when it's available, and that preference is worth knowing rather than assuming.

