## What If the Service Wasn't Good?

Yes, you should still tip. Figuring out <strong>how much to tip for a pedicure</strong> that didn't go well is really a question about the percentage, not about whether to tip at all. A tip acknowledges the time and labour behind a service, even when the outcome wasn't exactly what was wanted, and dropping to zero is rarely the right response to a disappointing result.

Where the adjustment happens is in the percentage, not in whether a tip happens at all. Rushed service, an uneven shape, or a design that missed the reference photo brought in can reasonably bring a tip down toward 10-15% rather than the standard 20%. What shouldn't happen is treating the tip screen as the place to register a complaint. If something is genuinely wrong mid-service, the moment to speak up is during the appointment, while the technician still has the chance to fix it, not silently at checkout while quietly deciding to punish the tip instead.

This distinction matters because most disappointing pedicures are fixable in the moment and rarely fixable after the fact through a smaller tip. A technician told the shape is off, or the colour looks patchy, can often correct it before the appointment ends. A tip reduced after the fact fixes nothing and mostly leaves both people frustrated. Speaking up in real time, calmly and specifically, gets a better outcome than adjusting gratuity as a stand-in for feedback. For a deeper look at whether <a href='/blog/pedicure-tipping/is-it-rude-not-to-tip-pedicure'>is it rude to skip the tip entirely</a> after a bad experience, the short answer is that skipping it entirely is reserved for genuinely careless or unprofessional service, not for a design that simply wasn't loved.

## Tipping When Two Technicians Worked on You

Tip each technician separately, based on the portion of the service they performed, rather than combining both services into one tip and hoping it gets divided fairly.

This comes up most often during a mani-pedi appointment, when one technician handles feet and a second handles hands, or during a longer visit where a shift change happens partway through. The cleanest approach is to mentally, or literally, split the total service cost between the two portions of work, then apply the chosen percentage to each share individually. A $70 mani-pedi split evenly between two technicians means each one gets tipped on roughly $35 of service, not a single tip handed to whoever happens to be standing at the register at the end.

If the split isn't obvious, a simple $5 to $10 per technician for a standard service, adjusted up for anything more involved like gel application or nail art, keeps things fair without requiring an exact percentage calculation of a divided bill. What matters most is that both people who worked on the visit are acknowledged individually rather than assuming the front desk will sort out the division after the fact.

